<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Nu'man III ibn al-Mundhir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 602</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Nu'man III ibn al-Mundhir was the last Lakhmid king of al-Hira, reigning from about 582 until 602 as a client of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>. A Christian of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a> and a patron of poets such as al-Nabigha, he fell out with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a>, who had him imprisoned and put to death. The end of the Lakhmid buffer state weakened Persian control of the desert frontier and was followed by the Arab victory at Dhi Qar.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Al-Nu'man, often known by the patronymic Abu Qabus, was the son of al-Mundhir IV ibn al-Mundhir and Salma, the daughter of a Jewish goldsmith from Fadak who had been a slave of a man of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kalb">Banu Kalb</a>. Contemporary poets mocked him for his mother's servile origin, and the Arabic sources describe him as ugly, red-haired and small. According to al-Tabari he was raised by the Christian poet Adi ibn Zayd, whose family served the Sasanian king as secretaries for Arab affairs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His succession was contested, since the clan of the Banu Marina backed his half-brother al-Aswad. The Arabic sources say that Adi ibn Zayd arranged for the other sons to tell the Sasanian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormizd-iv">Hormizd IV</a> that they could control the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> except al-Nu'man, who then promised to control both the Arabs and his brothers. Hormizd made him king and gave him a crown worth 60,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a>. He faced feuds among his subject tribes, and his brother and son were once captured and ransomed for one thousand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>.</p><p>His only recorded campaign was an attack on the Byzantine fortress of Circesium during the Byzantine–Sasanian War of 572–591. Arab accounts say he sheltered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a> during his flight from the usurper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahram-chobin">Bahram Chobin</a> in 590. After Khosrow regained his throne the two fell out, perhaps after al-Nu'man executed Adi ibn Zayd, whose son then turned Khosrow against him. Al-Nu'man fled to the Banu Bakr but surrendered and, by one account, was crushed by elephants; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> chronicles tell of a trap at a feast or of poisoning.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Khosrow then removed the Lakhmids and gave al-Hira to Iyas ibn Qabisah al-Ta'i, ending a dynasty that had shielded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> against the Arab tribes for almost three centuries. The Bakr soon revolted and won at Dhi Qar, and these events heralded the Battle of Qadisiyya in 636. Irfan Shahîd notes that later histories counted his reign the most memorable after that of his grandfather al-Mundhir III. Al-Hira remained the chief Arab cultural centre of its time and a seat of a Nestorian bishopric.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Nu'man III ibn al-Mundhir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 602 Khosrow II deposed and executed al-<b>Nu'man III</b>, the last Lakhmid king, and replaced the dynasty with Persian governors.</blockquote><a href="/state/lakhmid-kingdom">Lakhmid Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hira">Al-Hirah</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.98,44.39&amp;year=592" aria-label="Al-Hirah on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Al-Nu'man III ibn al-Mundhir"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6033 4766h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6033 4766h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.98,44.39&amp;year=592">What was here in 592 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
